            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>987,653</cell>
              <cell>489,691</cell>
              <cell>497,942</cell>
              <cell>20</cell>
              <cell>244,669</cell>
              <cell>243,979</cell>
              <cell>690</cell>
              <cell>8,176.67</cell>
              <cell>120.79</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>2</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>3</cell>
              <cell>1,453,787</cell>
              <cell>704,089</cell>
              <cell>749,673</cell>
              <cell>25</cell>
              <cell>298,472</cell>
              <cell>297,990</cell>
              <cell>482</cell>
              <cell>12,553.27</cell>
              <cell>115.81</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>4</cell>
              <cell>2,162,202</cell>
              <cell>1,077,272</cell>
              <cell>1,084,835</cell>
              <cell>95</cell>
              <cell>616,046</cell>
              <cell>598,237</cell>
              <cell>17,809</cell>
              <cell>7,504.91</cell>
              <cell>288.11</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>5</cell>
              <cell>1,421,932</cell>
              <cell>710,707</cell>
              <cell>711,191</cell>
              <cell>34</cell>
              <cell>402,466</cell>
              <cell>399,523</cell>
              <cell>2,943</cell>
              <cell>6,037.27</cell>
              <cell>235.53</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
          Row 2 of the joined dataset has <c>NA</c> for all the census columns,
          because <c>TAITA TAVETA</c> didn't match the census name,
          <c>TAITA/TAVETA</c>.
          In fact, there are several counties in the accidents data that didn't
          match the census, and so have <c>NA</c> in all the census columns.
          This happened wherever the county names were recorded differently in
          the two datasets.
          We can find them by looking for crash rows where <c>Population</c> is
          <c>NA</c>:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    kenya_accidents_join %&gt;%
      filter(is.na(Population)) %&gt;%
      pull(COUNTY) %&gt;%
      unique()
    #&gt; [1] "TAITA TAVETA"    "NAIROBI"         "KERCHO"
    #&gt; [4] "MURANGA"         "MWINGI"          "ELGEYO MARAKWET"
    #&gt; [7] "MAKURU"          "NYAHURURU"        NA
    #&gt; [10] "MARAKWET"
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Each of these is a county name from the accidents data that didn't
          find a match in the census.
          If we compare them with the county names in the census data, we can
          see what went wrong:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Some are spelling differences: the accidents data used
                <c>NAIROBI</c> where the census uses <c>NAIROBI CITY</c>, and
                <c>TAITA TAVETA</c> where the census uses <c>TAITA/TAVETA</c>.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Some are errors carried forward from the original data:
                <c>KERCHO</c> and <c>MAKURU</c> were identified as typos for
                <c>KERICHO</c> and <c>NAKURU</c> back in
                <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                And some are towns that were recorded as counties when they are
                not: Nyahururu is a town in Laikipia County, and Mwingi is a
                town in Kitui County.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          For every one of these mismatches, R had no way of knowing that, for
          example, <c>NAIROBI</c> and <c>NAIROBI CITY</c> are the same place.
          The join key has to match character for character.
          One extra space, one slash, or one letter is enough for R to treat two
          values as completely different.
          We still have the crash data and the population data, but without an
          identical key, we can't connect them.
          So before we can join, we need to change those county names in the
          accidents data to match the census.
        </p>

        <p>
          We should also check whether any rows have <c>NA</c> as their county.
          These are crashes where nobody recorded the county at all:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
      filter(is.na(COUNTY))
          </code>
        </program>

        <table xml:id="table-ch6-na-county">
          <title>
            Rows with missing county values.
            The county can be inferred from the <c>BASE/SUB BASE</c> column:
            Nanyuki and Kinangop are both towns, not counties.
          </title>

          <tabular halign="left">
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
              <cell>Row</cell>
              <cell>TIME</cell>
              <cell>BASE/SUB BASE</cell>
              <cell>COUNTY</cell>
              <cell>ROAD</cell>
              <cell>PLACE</cell>
              <cell>BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS</cell>
              <cell>GENDER</cell>
              <cell>AGE</cell>
              <cell>CAUSE CODE</cell>
              <cell>VICTIM</cell>
              <cell>NO.</cell>
              <cell>Date</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>2205</cell>
              <cell>NANYUKI</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>KINAMBA</cell>
              <cell>NGARUA</cell>
              <cell>The vehicle overturned killing 6 victims</cell>
              <cell>4F &amp; INF</cell>
              <cell>A</cell>
              <cell>26</cell>
              <cell>PASSENGER</cell>
              <cell>6</cell>
              <cell>2016-04-08</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>2</cell>
              <cell>2142</cell>
              <cell>KINANGOP</cell>
              <cell>NA</cell>
              <cell>NJAMBINI OLKALAO</cell>
              <cell>KAMAGUTA</cell>
              <cell>The vehicle hit a pedal cyclist</cell>
              <cell>M</cell>
              <cell>33</cell>
              <cell>98</cell>
              <cell>P/CYCLIST</cell>
              <cell>1</cell>
              <cell>2016-04-08</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
          Both rows are missing a county, but neither one is really a mystery.
          The <c>BASE/SUB BASE</c> column tells us where each crash was reported
          from: Nanyuki is a town in Laikipia County, and Kinangop is a
          sub-county in Nyandarua County.
          So we can fill in the missing counties using <c>BASE/SUB BASE</c>, at
          the same time as fixing all the county names we found above:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
  mutate(COUNTY = case_when(
    
    # Spelling and formatting corrections to match census names
    COUNTY == "TAITA TAVETA"    ~ "TAITA/TAVETA",
    COUNTY == "NAIROBI"         ~ "NAIROBI CITY",
    COUNTY == "ELGEYO MARAKWET" ~ "ELGEYO/MARAKWET",
    COUNTY == "MARAKWET"        ~ "ELGEYO/MARAKWET",
    COUNTY == "MURANGA"         ~ "MURANG'A",
    
    # Corrections carried forward from data cleaning in Chapter 4
    COUNTY == "MAKURU"          ~ "NAKURU",
    COUNTY == "KERCHO"          ~ "KERICHO",
    
    # Towns incorrectly recorded as counties
    # Nanyuki and Nyahururu are both towns in Laikipia County
    `BASE/SUB BASE` == "NANYUKI"         ~ "LAIKIPIA",
    COUNTY == "NYAHURURU"       ~ "LAIKIPIA",
    # Mwingi is a town in Kitui County
    COUNTY == "MWINGI"          ~ "KITUI",
    # Kinangop is a sub-county in Nyandarua County
    `BASE/SUB BASE` == "KINANGOP"        ~ "NYANDARUA",
    
    # Everything else stays as is
    TRUE ~ COUNTY
  ))

  # Confirm no remaining NA counties
    kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
      filter(is.na(COUNTY)) %&gt;%
      nrow()
    #&gt; [1] 0
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          We can also check the other direction: which census counties don't
          have any crashes in the accidents data?
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    setdiff(unique(kenya_census_2019$County),
            unique(kenya_accidents_2016$COUNTY))
    #&gt; [1] "TANA RIVER"    "LAMU"          "WAJIR"
    #&gt; [4] "MANDERA"       "MARSABIT"      "ISIOLO"
    #&gt; [7] "THARAKA-NITHI" "TURKANA"       "WEST POKOT"
    #&gt; [10] "SAMBURU"
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          We'd expect this second list.
          These are counties with no recorded fatal crashes in this three-month
          extract, which is plausible for counties that are sparsely populated
          or remote.
          It's the first list that needed fixing.
          Now we've applied all the county name corrections, let's check that
          there are no mismatches left:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    setdiff(unique(kenya_accidents_2016$COUNTY),
            unique(kenya_census_2019$County))
    #&gt; character(0)
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Every county name in the accidents data now has an exact match in the
          census.
          Only now does it make sense to run the join again.
        </p>

        <paragraphs>
          <title>Choosing the right join: left_join</title>

          <p>
            With the county names fixed, we can run the join again.
            But we still need to think about which kind of join to use.
            A <c>full_join()</c> keeps every row from both datasets:
          </p>

          <program language="r">
            <code>
      kenya_accidents_join &lt;- full_join(kenya_accidents_2016,
                                         kenya_census_2019,
                                         by = c("COUNTY" = "County"))

      nrow(kenya_accidents_2016)
      #&gt; [1] 378

      nrow(kenya_accidents_join)
      #&gt; [1] 388
            </code>
          </program>

          <p>
            We've gained 10 rows!
            These are counties that appear in the census data but not in our
            incidents data, including Tana River, Lamu, and Wajir.
            The `full_join()` has included these counties, with `NA` values for
            all the accident-related columns.
            (Note that just because a county doesn't appear in our accidents
            data doesn't mean there have never been any crashes there.
            It simply means that no fatal crashes were recorded in our dataset
            during this three-month period).
            Since we're interested in the counties in our accidents data, we
            don't need to add these extra rows.
            Instead, we can use a <c>left_join()</c> to keep all the counties
            from our accidents data and bring in the corresponding census
            information.
          </p>

          <p>
            <c>left_join()</c> keeps every row from the left dataset (the
            accidents data), brings in the matching information from the right
            dataset (the census), and throws away any rows on the right that
            don't have a match on the left.
            In other words, every crash gets population data attached, and no
            new rows are added.
          </p>

          <program language="r">
            <code>
      kenya_accidents_join &lt;- left_join(kenya_accidents_2016,
                                         kenya_census_2019,
                                         by = c("COUNTY" = "County"))

      nrow(kenya_accidents_join)
      #&gt; [1] 378
            </code>
          </program>

          <p>
            We're back to 378 rows.
            The first five rows of the joined dataset are below.
            Notice that row 2, which is <c>TAITA TAVETA</c> and didn't match
            before, now shows <c>TAITA/TAVETA</c>, with a full set of population
            values.
          </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <table xml:id="table-ch6-left-joined-sample">
          <title>
            First five rows after the corrected left join.
            Every row now has population data attached.
            Row 2 previously failed to match; after correcting the county name
            to <c>TAITA/TAVETA</c> it now joins successfully.
          </title>

          <tabular halign="left">
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
              <cell>Row</cell>
              <cell>TIME</cell>
              <cell>BASE</cell>
              <cell>COUNTY</cell>
              <cell>ROAD</cell>
